; ============================================================
; TITLE:    POLY Gallery
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     turtle geometry, polygons, stars, total turning
; SUMMARY:  The famous POLY procedure from Turtle Geometry draws six different figures from just two numbers.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Draws a gallery of six figures in a grid: a triangle, a square,
;   a five-pointed star, a nine-pointed star and two more. Every one
;   of them is made by the same tiny procedure, POLY, with a different
;   side and angle.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Then type your own, for example  poly 100 135  or
;   poly 80 160  in the command center (clean first if you like).
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a procedure with two inputs
;   * repeating "forward, turn" is all a polygon really is
;   * the total turning of a closed path is a multiple of 360
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Try angles that do not divide 360 evenly, like 100 or 170.
;   * Change the gallery list at the bottom to show your favorites.
;   * What happens with an angle of 1? Of 359?
; ============================================================

; In Abelson and diSessa's book Turtle Geometry (1981) the very first
; program is POLY:
;     TO POLY :SIDE :ANGLE
;       FORWARD :SIDE  RIGHT :ANGLE
;       POLY :SIDE :ANGLE
;     END
; It never stops. The turtle just keeps going round its figure. The book
; then shows that the figure closes once the turtle has turned through a
; whole number of full turns (360, 720, 1080 ...). That idea is used here
; so our POLY stops by itself, which lets us draw many figures in a row.

to poly :side :angle
  localmake "turned 0
  ; DO.UNTIL runs the body at least once, then checks if we are home.
  do.until [
    forward :side
    right :angle
    make "turned :turned + :angle
  ] [(remainder :turned 360) = 0]
end

; Draw one figure centred roughly in a cell of the gallery.
; The start point is shifted left and up by about half the side (the turtle turns
; right, so the figure hangs below its starting edge) so
; the figure sits in the middle of its cell.
to figure :x :y :side :angle :color
  penup
  setxy :x - :side / 2 :y + :side / 2
  setheading 90
  pendown
  setpencolor :color
  poly :side :angle
  ; Write the two numbers under the figure, so the reader can see
  ; that only the inputs changed.
  penup
  setxy :x - 30 :y - 115
  setheading 0
  label (sentence "poly :side :angle)
end

to gallery
  clearscreen
  hideturtle
  setbackground [250 246 235]
  setpensize 3
  setlabelheight 16
  figure -260 120 110 120 "#4169e1
  figure 0 120 100 90 "#2e8b57
  figure 260 120 120 144 "#dc143c
  figure -260 -150 110 160 "#ff8c00
  figure 0 -150 70 60 "#8040b0
  figure 260 -150 120 135 "#008080
end

gallery
